  "chatScriptBestPractices": {
    "messages": [
      {
        "id": "1",
        "role": "user",
        "content": "Best practices for structuring course modules",
        "citations": []
      },
      {
        "id": "2",
        "role": "agent",
        "content": "Here are the key best practices for structuring effective course modules.\n\nKeep each module focused on a single learning objective. Learners retain information better when content is scoped tightly — avoid combining multiple concepts in one module unless they are tightly related.\n\nStart with a clear outcome statement. Let learners know upfront what they will be able to do by the end. This sets expectations and activates prior knowledge before new material is introduced.\n\nSequence content from concrete to abstract. Begin with examples, demonstrations, or scenarios before introducing underlying theory. Learners build stronger mental models when they have something tangible to anchor new concepts to.\n\nBuild in retrieval checkpoints. Short quizzes, reflection prompts, or application tasks spaced throughout the module significantly improve long-term retention compared to passive reading alone.\n\nEnd with a synthesis activity. A brief summary task — such as explaining the core idea in their own words or applying it to a new scenario — consolidates learning and signals module completion clearly.",
        "citations": []
      }
    ]
  },

